nodecode-epistemic is a rewrite of quangphucqp's quangphucqp/epistemic-harness:
https://github.com/quangphucqp/epistemic-harness
commit 5abd0dc482096c3f64379115fc79cc96e4e7203a
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Epistemic Harness contributors (LICENSE)

The rewrite keeps the prediction-then-observation loop: a case with a decision,
the unknown that could change it and a stopping condition; a check committed
with what it is expected to show before the tool call runs; the observation
compared against that prediction, with a belief change recorded separately; a
mismatch marking the claim stale until a revision addresses it; and a close that
cannot happen while a check is pending. It also keeps the one thing the original
did quietly — the committed tool's own result is kept, so the comparison is
against what came back rather than a memory of it — and the reminder put in
front of the model while a case runs. It is rewritten in Common Lisp as one
Nodecode cell: the two tools (epistemic_case, epistemic_probe) became two
declared contracts, the injected state became one bounded line at each request,
and the case store is one S-expression file under the home.

Left out, and why: the optional hosted method consultation (epistemic_methods,
a TypeSafe/Jev ranking of candidate investigation methods) — it is off by
default in the original, needs a vendor key, and Nodecode's TypeSafe traffic
already has cells of its own. Also left out: the multi-file journaled store with
`fcntl` leases, write-ahead journals and replay validation, which buys
crash-recovery guarantees a personal case record does not need (this store writes
one file and replaces it by rename); the subagent-stop and session-reset hooks;
and the probe "recover" verb for an interrupted run, which `discard` stands in
for.
